= BARK: a successor to the nbtree index access method =

''Status:'' a working access method under development, at parity with nbtree
on most of its scalar workload (see [[#Where BARK stands|Where BARK
stands]]); the multikey (M) series is being built. This document is the
contract every commit refers to: the contracts, the decisions and the
roadmap. How the code works is in <code>src/backend/access/bark/README</code>,
and the user manual is <code>doc/src/sgml/bark.sgml</code>. Update this
document in the same commit that changes a contract; do not let code and
this document drift.

== What BARK is ==

BARK is a new index access method (<code>CREATE INDEX ... USING bark</code>),
intended over time to be a drop-in successor to <code>nbtree</code>: it keeps
every nbtree capability (ordered scans, uniqueness, multicolumn keys, INCLUDE
columns, deduplication, index-only scans, parallel build and scan, the
amcheck contract) and adds capabilities the B-tree shape cannot express
cheaply:

* '''Arbitrary-size keys and values.''' nbtree caps a key at roughly a third
  of a page and stores no payload beyond the heap TID (and INCLUDE columns).
  BARK stores keys and values whose size is bounded by the index, not by the
  page, in the tradition of Berkeley DB.
* '''Sorted duplicates.''' A key may map to many values held in sorted order,
  again in the libdb tradition, rather than to a single heap pointer plus a
  deduplicated TID posting list.
* '''An inverted / posting-list paradigm''' as a first-class shape, so an
  entry's "value" side can be a set of row locators. This is the GIN/RUM
  paradigm brought under one AM (it is what OrioleDB's and DocumentDB's
  inverted indexes need), rather than a separate AM per shape.
* '''Multithread-readiness.''' No process-global mutable state on the read or
  write path (BARK's C files have no file-scope variables; per-index state
  lives in the relcache's <code>rd_amcache</code> and per-statement state in
  <code>ii_AmCache</code>). The annotations of the multithreaded PostgreSQL
  work (the <code>xtc</code> worktree's <code>utils/global_lifetime.h</code>)
  are not in this tree.

BARK is '''not''' a replacement for the catalog and TOAST indexes. Those stay
on nbtree by deliberate policy (see [[#The capability sweep|the capability
sweep]]); BARK targets user indexes.

=== Where BARK stands ===

'''Parity with nbtree.''' BARK has nbtree's tree and its algorithms: Lehman
and Yao descent and move-right with lock coupling across splits, incomplete
splits finished by later writers, split points chosen by bytes and
fillfactor, suffix truncation, heap TID order among equal keys (nbtree's
heapkeyspace), bottom-up deletion, a merge of a leaf's equal-key entries
before a split (nbtree's deduplication pass), VACUUM in one physical pass
with cycle IDs and cleanup locks, page deletion with safe recycling, its own
WAL resource manager with hot-standby conflict handling, the rightmost-leaf
fast path, page-at-a-time scans with nbtree's pin rule, mark/restore,
parallel builds, scans and VACUUM, required array keys, skip scan, per-page
satisfied keys, row-comparison positioning, cross-type keys, NULLS NOT
DISTINCT, speculative insertion, btcostestimate's cost model, an operator
class for every btree class, and nbtree's tools (amcheck with
heapallindexed and the parent check, pg_amcheck, pageinspect, pgstattuple).
Beyond nbtree it has keys of any size, LIST and POSTING entries, leaf prefix
compression and KNN ordering.

'''Not yet at parity.''' No PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE constraint can use a BARK
index, so there is no deferrable uniqueness (U1, held). IS NULL and IS NOT
NULL only filter (N2, held). There are no dead-tuple hints from scans and no
simple deletion before a split (K2), and skip scan runs forward only and
over one column; both wait on <code>amgetbatch</code> (cf/4351, G1). CLUSTER
and REPACK read through a BARK index rather than sorting
(<code>tuplesort_begin_cluster</code> asserts btree). The RI trigger fast
path is btree-only. A multikey index cannot be scanned backward after a
forward start, in parallel when it needs a seen set, or in the order of an
operator on its extracted column, until M1d.

'''Performance.''' From the Z2 benchmark (BENCHMARK-2026-10b.md): idxam at
5d4e2883b10, release build (<code>-O3</code>, no assertions), EC2
c7i.4xlarge (16 vCPU), Debian 12, shared_buffers 4GB, autovacuum off; 5
runs, the first dropped, median of the other four; pgbench <code>-M
prepared</code> 30 s. The ratio is BARK's median over btree's: below 1 is
better for BARK for times and sizes, above 1 for tps. The report marks a
row within noise when the two ranges overlap.

