For reference, on the same data directory: btree(a) has a bitmap node of
21.0 ms (''one'') and 0.19 ms (kc), and an index-only scan of 66.5 ms
(''one''). SINGLE-only BARK has a bitmap node of 31.0 ms (''one''). Neither
change alters a page or a WAL byte. The batch-decode binary returned the
same <code>sum(b)</code> and 1000000 distinct ctids as a sequential scan.
Nothing reads bytes 8-15 of the sbm serialization header:
<code>sbm_deserialize</code> skips them, and grep finds no other reader. Yet
<code>sbm_add</code> invalidates the cardinality cache, so every POSTING
insert walked every chunk once to fill in an unused field. The decode cost
was the per-call chunk walk in <code>sbm_next_member</code>, not the
encoding: with batches, a POSTING set decodes 2-3x faster per locator than
btree, and 4x faster than BARK's own SINGLE entries.

So the measurements point away from new structures. Generic WAL is half of
every insert, whatever shape the entry has. LIST appends cost WAL volume.
POSTING appends and POSTING scans cost two sbm calls that do avoidable work.
No measured cost scales with how many entries a key has.

'''Options.'''

; (a) Nothing beyond what is merged.
: Cost: none. Keeps hot-key insert at 3-8x btree (run 2), ks WAL at 9.4x
  btree, POSTING bitmap scans at 3-3.6x btree's node time, and CREATE INDEX
  (before option (f)) at 3.2x btree's size. Touches nothing.

; (b) A GIN-style posting tree per key.
: A TID-keyed B-tree rooted in the same relation, with sbm-compressed leaf
  segments, referenced from the key's one main-tree entry. GIN's version
  (<code>gindatapage.c</code>) splits each leaf into varbyte segments of at
  most 384 bytes (target 256) and re-encodes only the segment an insert
  touches. Its WAL record carries only the added TIDs
  (<code>GIN_SEGMENT_ADDITEMS</code>), and varbyte never grows on removal.
  It also needs its own page type, Lehman and Yao descent and move-right, a
  two-pass VACUUM (leaf pass, then cleanup-locked page deletion from the
  root), a predicate lock on the posting-tree root, and a custom WAL rmgr.
: Benefit: locators in order within a key, and a re-encode bounded by one
  segment. After the cardinality-hint fix, the sbm work left in
  <code>bark_posting_add_tid</code> is about 8% of hot-key insert, which is
  all that bounding the re-encode can win back. Order within a key matters
  only for an exact (key, locator) lookup: retail delete, and C-DELETE's
  <code>amdeletemark</code>. That is better solved the way nbtree solved it
  (heap TID as the final key attribute, with posting splits), and it is
  needed for SINGLE entries too. Done: leaf entries are ordered by (key, heap
  TID) since on-disk version 2, with pivots that keep a heap TID and entries
  divided around a TID that falls inside them (README, "Heap TID order").
: Cost: a second tree protocol inside one AM, at the moment the concurrency,
  scan, VACUUM and split work is making the one tree correct. Every one of
  them would need a posting-tree version: VACUUM cycle IDs, deleted-page
  handling, the page-at-a-time scan position, split-point choice. Rejected.

; (c) A WiredTiger-style per-page delta, merged when the page fills.
: WiredTiger never changes a disk page image in place. New keys go into
  in-memory skiplists hung off the page (<code>WT_INSERT_HEAD</code>, one
  per on-disk slot), and reconciliation, at eviction or checkpoint, merges
  them into a new page image. PostgreSQL pages are updated in place in
  shared buffers, so the skiplist has no counterpart here, and a structure
  outside the page would need WAL of its own. The part that does carry over
  is the merge itself: new locators go onto the page as plain SINGLE
  entries, and when the page fills, one pass folds each key's run into its
  LIST or POSTING entry. This is exactly <code>_bt_dedup_pass</code>, and
  nbtree's numbers above say it costs nothing.
: For BARK the pass replaces the per-insert coalesce in
  <code>bark_coalesce_list</code>. An insert becomes a SINGLE insert: measured
  at 0.6-0.76 of bark(a)'s time on kc and 0.23-0.58 on ks, with WAL at
  0.94-1.1x (kc) and 0.14-0.31x (ks) of bark(a)'s (runs 2 and 5). Then one
  page rewrite per page fill is added. At nbtree's rate (one pass per 122
  inserts), with the pass logged as a full-page delta under generic WAL, the
  estimate is about 0.65 GB of WAL per 10M rows. That is an estimate, not a
  measurement. It still puts ks near 2.3 GB, against 11.6 GB now and btree's
  1.2 GB. Index size should stay at the retail sizes or drop below them. Each
  pass sorts a merged run's locators, so locators are in order within an
  entry, and it merges a run's several small entries into one, which the
  per-insert path never does.
: Touches: <code>barkinsert.c</code> (drop the coalesce call; call the pass
  where a leaf would split), a new <code>barkdedup.c</code>, and
  <code>bark_allequalimage</code> as the gate, the rule insert coalescing
  already follows. A run merged into a POSTING is sized for its removal
  bound, as today. Like nbtree's pass, it keeps a run as several entries
  when one entry would exceed <code>BarkMaxItemSize</code>.
: ''Status:'' the pass was built as <code>bark_merge_page</code> in
  <code>barkdelete.c</code> (7a80942793b), beside the per-insert coalesce
  rather than in place of it; see recommendation 4.

; (d) A GIN-fastupdate-style pending list.
: Unsorted pending pages hang off the metapage and are merged into the tree
  when they pass <code>gin_pending_list_limit</code> (4 MB), or by VACUUM.
  Every scan reads the whole list. SSI turns every scan into a lock on the
  metapage, which means the whole index. Ordered scans would need a merge
  with the list. On the hot-key test (run 2), GIN with fastupdate was slower
  than without (18.5 vs 12.2 s) and wrote 1.8x the WAL. BARK's whole reason
  to exist is ordered scans, and a pending list gives them up. Rejected.

; (e) A BARK WAL resource manager instead of generic WAL.
: Generic WAL copies the page twice and diffs it
  (<code>computeRegionDelta</code>) on every change: 50-55% of insert CPU for
  every shape, which no value-set structure can touch. A BARK rmgr would log
  what changed, as nbtree does: insert one tuple, add locators to an entry,
  split, dedup pass, vacuum, page deletion. It also gives BARK something
  generic WAL cannot. <code>generic_redo</code> resolves no recovery
  conflicts, but reusing a deleted page needs one (nbtree's
  <code>XLOG_BTREE_REUSE_PAGE</code>, GiST's
  <code>gistXLogPageReuse</code>), or a hot-standby scan can follow a link
  into a recycled page. Moves: insert CPU toward SINGLE-only UNLOGGED (kc
  7.2 s, btree 3.2 s, run 2), and WAL toward btree's for every shape.
: Cost: the largest of the options. An rmgr id, a redo routine per record,
  <code>rm_desc</code>, <code>rm_identify</code>, <code>rm_mask</code> for
  <code>wal_consistency_checking</code>, and a rewrite of every
  <code>GenericXLogStart</code> site (13 in <code>bark.c</code>,
  <code>barkinsert.c</code> and <code>barkutils.c</code>). Each of those sites
  is being changed right now by the concurrency, VACUUM and split work.
: ''Status:'' done (92c15cee8a5, 03fb7f146bb, ab351f6ea3f, ec2a4ef1ac4,
  a45e6d67a7c); BARK writes no generic WAL. See recommendation 5.

; (f) Build LIST and POSTING entries at CREATE INDEX.
: <code>bark_load</code> receives tuples sorted by key and then heap TID
  (<code>tuplesort_begin_index_btree</code> breaks ties on
  <code>t_tid</code>), so it can group each equal-key run, as nbtree's
  <code>_bt_load</code> does when it deduplicates, and emit SINGLE, LIST or
  POSTING through the same chooser as insert. It applies insert's
  <code>bark_allequalimage</code> gate. Done; see run 6 below.
: Run 6 (release, serial build, three alternating rounds, CREATE INDEX
  time / pages):
