'''M-series -- documents (multikey)'''

These make BARK an index that a document store such as DocumentDB can use in
place of an inverted AM, without changing the scalar design above.

* '''M1''' -- multikey keys: an opclass support procedure that extracts N
  keys from one column value, called by insert and build; one row's sorted
  keys are inserted in one pass that reuses the descent. A scan returns a row
  that matches through several keys once. The meta page records whether the
  index has multikey rows. Needs H1, so that (key, TID) names exactly one
  entry member. Design: [[#M1: multikey keys|M1: multikey keys]] below.
  ''(in progress: design 76d2f802ab2; M1a catalog interface done,
  36e18a3ca93; M1b insert and build done, ac12fa1e140; M1c scans done, the
  M1c commit on bark-m1c (see "As built" under "Scans"); M1d planner to
  follow)''
* '''M2''' -- operator classes on RUM's model. BARK takes GIN's support
  functions for extracted keys (extract a value's keys; extract a query's
  keys, with partial match; consistent) and RUM's ordering functions
  (ordering and outer ordering), so a RUM or GIN operator class ports to
  BARK with little change. RUM's per-locator data functions (config, which
  declares the per-locator type, and the join of per-locator values) are
  not taken: M3 is closed, and a class that attaches a column to its keys
  becomes a composite key, the column after the extracted one, ordered by
  M2's KNN scan inside a key's run ("M3: per-locator data" below).
  Plus classes matching RUM's for arrays (containment, overlap, equality,
  similarity ordering) and for scalar types with RUM's distance operators,
  which BARK's KNN scan already serves on a leading column. The new
  procedures are numbered after btree's, since BARK's scalar classes live in
  btree's operator families; <code>barkvalidate</code> checks them. RUM's
  tsvector classes are out of scope: their ranking needs per-occurrence
  positions and belongs to a text-search index (see "Containment
  queries").
  BARK also takes the query interface of DocumentDB's pg_extended_btree,
  which suits an ordered index better than GIN's: an extract-value function
  (a column value to its keys), an extract-query function that turns a
  query operator and argument into a sorted set of disjoint key ranges
  ("boundaries", each a lower and an upper bound with its strategy: = is a
  point, $gt a half-open range, $in a set of points) plus data for a
  recheck, and an index-recheck function that tests an index entry against
  that data and answers true, false or maybe (maybe leaves the row to the
  heap recheck). A scan walks the ranges in order, as BARK's array scan
  walks an IN-list, re-descending only where the next range starts past the
  page. An operator class may provide either interface; one written for
  pg_extended_btree ports to BARK unchanged. ''(blocked on M1c's scans;
  barkvalidate accepts procedures 7-14 since 36e18a3ca93)''
* '''M3''' -- extra data per locator (RUM's addInfo). ''Closed, not
  needed'': a composite key (key, value) holds the same members in the same
  order, and every use case found is served by one. What both would need,
  an ordering operator on the column after the extracted one inside a
  key's run, is M2's. Design note:
  [[#M3: per-locator data (closed)|M3: per-locator data]] below.
* '''M4''' -- unique indexes over multikey keys with document-store
  semantics: unique across rows, while one row may repeat a key.
  ''(designed; not started.  Design: [[#M4: unique indexes over multikey keys|M4]] below)''
* '''M5''' -- wildcard indexes, as MongoDB's: a test-module operator class
  over <code>jsonb</code> whose extract-value procedure (7) emits one key
  (path, value) for every path that the column's projection keeps, and one
  per element under a path that holds an array; the projection (include or
  exclude a list of paths) is an option of the class (procedure 5). Its
  extract-query procedure (8) turns a qual on one path into boundaries
  inside that path's range, and an ordering operator on a path sorts by it
  with MongoDB's array rule. Markers record which paths have held an array,
  so a query on a path that never has needs no seen set (an M5 extension of
  procedure 8 tells the scan so). A compound index (fixed columns, then the
  wildcard column) keeps (fixed columns, path, value). Needs M1 and M2's
  procedures 7-9; unique wildcard indexes need M4. Nothing in it changes
  the on-disk format; "Wildcard indexes as the check" under M1 is its
  design. ''(in progress: M5a, the wildcard operator class module, against
  M1a's interface; roadmap entry f24f7e10cab)''
* '''T1''' -- term containment at scale; see "Containment queries" under
  C-KEYSHAPE. ''(not started; the TID filter for an ordered scan is blocked
  on cf/4351)''

=== M1: multikey keys ===

The design of record for M1; a card implementing it should not have to
re-decide anything here. An index column whose operator class extracts
several keys from one value is an ''extracted column''. A row has one entry
per distinct key of that column, each with the row's heap TID; every other
key column (a ''fixed column'') has the same value in all of them. With H1,
(key, TID) names exactly one entry member, and everything below relies on
that.

==== Operator classes ====

'''Families.''' A multikey operator class lives in an operator family of
BARK's own (<code>CREATE OPERATOR FAMILY ... USING bark</code>; a
<code>CREATE OPERATOR CLASS ... USING bark</code> that names no btree family
already gets one), never in a btree family, for two reasons.
<code>pg_amproc</code> rows carry no access method, so a support procedure
numbered past btree's six in a btree family makes btvalidate report the
family invalid. And the planner reads a btree family as a sort order on the
column's values (pathkeys, merge joins, row comparisons, equivalence
classes); an extracted column's keys are ordered, its values are not. A
column is extracted exactly when its class's family belongs to BARK and has
support procedure 7. Fixed columns use btree families, as every BARK column
does today.

'''Support procedures.''' Numbered after btree's 1-6, so the scalar classes
keep btree's numbering. <code>amsupport</code> becomes 14, not 103 as in
pg_extended_btree: relcache allocates <code>amsupport</code> FmgrInfo per
column of every BARK index, scalar ones included.

* '''1''' compare, <code>int4 (key, key)</code>, required. Orders the keys,
  as GIN's compare procedure does, and is registered under the class's input
  type as GIN's are; so tuplesort, bark_build_keyinfo and every descent use
  it unchanged. barkvalidate checks its arguments against the key type, as
  ginvalidate does. CREATE OPERATOR CLASS registers a comparator under its
  own argument types for an ordering AM such as BARK, so a multikey class
  writes <code>FUNCTION 1 (input type, input type)</code>; barkvalidate
  reports a comparator registered under the key type with a hint saying
  so.
* '''2, 4, 5, 6''' sortsupport, equalimage, options, skipsupport: optional,
  btree's meanings, over the key type. Equalimage (4) is what lets the
  column's keys coalesce into LIST and POSTING entries
  (bark_allequalimage). Options (5) become BARK's
  <code>amoptsprocnum</code> (it was 0), for per-column options such as M5's
  projection. In_range (3) is not allowed on an extracted column.
* '''7''' extract-value, <code>internal (value, internal nkeys, internal
  nullFlags, internal markers, internal nmarkers)</code>, required; returns
  the key array. Two to five arguments: pg_extended_btree's procedure 101
  has the first two, GIN's extractValue the first three, and the last two
  return marker keys (see "Multikey metadata"). Keys may come in any order
  and repeat; BARK sorts them with procedure 1 and removes duplicates, as
  ginExtractEntries does.
* '''8''' extract-query, <code>void (query, int2 strategy, internal
  boundaries, internal nboundaries, internal extra, internal recheck [,
  internal flags])</code>, required for scans: pg_extended_btree's
  procedure 102, plus an optional seventh argument, a
  <code>BarkQueryFlags *</code> (bark.h) that BARK zeroes before the call.
  A class written for pg_extended_btree declares six arguments and never
  sees it; barkvalidate accepts six or seven. Its fields are
  <code>backward</code>, which an ordering operator sets to walk its
  boundaries backward (see "Ordered scans"), and <code>searchnulls</code>,
  which asks the scan to read the NULL entries too (a NULL value, an empty
  value or a NULL element), at the position the column's NULLS option gives
  them, and to return those rows with <code>xs_recheck</code> set, as
  GIN's INCLUDE_EMPTY search mode does. Fields are only appended (M5 adds
  "this scan needs no seen set"). It turns an operator's strategy and argument into
  boundaries, each a lower and an upper search element (an argument of the
  key type and a strategy 1-5; either may be absent, meaning unbounded; a
  point has = in both). BARK sorts the boundaries and raises an error if two
  intersect, as pg_extended_btree does; none means nothing matches.
  <code>extra</code> is the class's data for procedure 9, and
  <code>recheck</code> asks BARK to call procedure 9 on every entry in a
  boundary.
* '''9''' index-recheck, <code>int2 (key, int2 strategy, internal
  extra)</code>, required with 8: pg_extended_btree's procedure 103. Returns
  0 (true), 1 (maybe) or 2 (false) for one entry; maybe returns the row
  with <code>xs_recheck</code> set.
* '''10-13''' GIN's extractQuery, consistent, comparePartial and
  triConsistent (GIN's 3, 4, 5, 6), with GIN's signatures. barkvalidate
  accepts them from M1, so catalogs need not change; scans use them from M2.
* '''14''' fetch, <code>value (key)</code>, optional and reserved:
  barkvalidate accepts it, BARK does not call it (see "Index-only scans"
  under "Scans": a key cannot be turned back into its row's value).

'''Which interface first.''' BARK implements pg_extended_btree's (7-9) in
M1. Its queries are key ranges in key order, which BARK's scan already walks:
the leading-column array scan's re-descent, the bounds, skip scan. GIN's maps
onto it in M2. ExtractQuery's keys become point boundaries, and a
partial-match key a boundary from the key upward that ends where
comparePartial returns more than 0. BARK reads each boundary's TIDs, which
H1 keeps in TID order within a point's run, merges the streams by TID as
ginget.c merges posting lists (a partial-match range is not in TID order and
goes into a TIDBitmap first, as GIN's does), and calls triConsistent, or
consistent, with each TID's per-key presence vector. That is a bitmap scan:
GIN classes get no ordered scans. GIN's INCLUDE_EMPTY and ALL search modes
read the NULL entries (see "Insert and build"). A class provides 8 and 9,
or 10 and 11 or 13, or both; one written for pg_extended_btree ports by
renumbering 101-103 as 7-9.

'''Strategies.''' An operator in a BARK family has whatever strategy number
its class gives it, as in GIN; the number is only passed to procedures 8 and
9. <code>amstrategies</code> becomes 0, so CREATE OPERATOR CLASS accepts any
<code>int2</code>; barkvalidate keeps checking 1-5 and the KNN strategy 6 in
btree families, and barkadjustmembers keeps CREATE OPERATOR CLASS refusing,
in a btree family, the strategies past 5 and support procedures past 6 that
it refused before (such a member would make btvalidate report btree's own
classes in the family invalid). bark_translate_strategy returns
COMPARE_INVALID for a BARK family, so the planner never reads
<code>=</code> or <code>&lt;</code> semantics into its operators.
<code>amstorage</code> becomes true: the key type is the class's
<code>STORAGE</code> type (opckeytype), which index.c already gives the
column's attribute; a btree-family class must keep its input type, and
barkadjustmembers refuses a <code>STORAGE</code> clause for one, as
CREATE OPERATOR CLASS did before (the catalog's <code>name_ops</code> keeps
btree's cstring).

'''Columns.''' At most one extracted column per index, in any key position;
CREATE INDEX raises an error for two. Two would store the cross product of
their keys, N times M entries per row, which is why MongoDB refuses compound
indexes over two array fields ("parallel arrays"). An INCLUDE column is
never extracted.

==== Insert and build ====

'''Keys of a row.''' bark_insert calls procedure 7 on the extracted
column's value in a short-lived memory context, reset per row, as GIN's
<code>funcCtx</code> is. It sorts the keys with procedure 1 and removes
duplicates, then forms one entry per key with the row's fixed columns and
heap TID. A key that procedure 7 flags NULL becomes one NULL key. A NULL
value (procedure 7 is not called) and a value with no keys (an empty array)
also become one entry with a NULL key, as GIN's NULL_ITEM and EMPTY_ITEM
placeholders do, so every row has at least one entry and a scan that does
not constrain the extracted column still sees every row. BARK has a single
NULL category, not GIN's three, so a NULL entry means "NULL value, empty
value, or a NULL element", and a scan that has to tell these apart returns
the row with <code>xs_recheck</code> set (see "Scans").

'''One pass.''' The sorted keys are inserted in ascending (key, TID) order,
and the descent is shared between them. The first key descends as an
insert does today. After key ''i'' is placed on leaf L, still
exclusive-locked, key ''i''+1 goes:

* on L, when L is rightmost or (key ''i''+1, TID) sorts before L's high
  key; its offset is searched from the offset of key ''i'' onward;
* else on L's right sibling R: lock R, then release L, as
  bark_insert_stepright does (deleted and half-dead pages are stepped over,
  incomplete splits finished), when R is rightmost or the key sorts before
  R's high key;
* else by a new descent from the root, after releasing every lock.

All locks are taken left to right, one level at a time, as every BARK writer
takes them, so the walk cannot deadlock. The stack from the last descent is
kept across steps right; a split's parent insert finds its parent through
bark_getstackbuf, which moves right from a stale stack entry, as nbtree's
_bt_stepright relies on. A key whose insert splits its leaf
(bark_split releases both halves) is followed by a new descent.
CheckForSerializableConflictIn runs once for each leaf, before the first
key goes on it. Each key goes through the code that places a single entry
today: coalescing, bottom-up deletion, the single-value cut, the split. That
code moves out of bark_insert into a per-key function that both callers
use, and an OVERSIZED key gets its own overflow chain as now.

'''WAL.''' M1 reuses the records a single-key insert writes: INSERT_LEAF,
ADD_TID, OVERWRITE or INSERT_SWAP, one per key, and SPLIT for a split. No
new record type and no change to redo. Each key's change is atomic by
itself, and a crash between two keys of a row is harmless: the row's heap
tuple belongs to the same uncommitted transaction, so the keys already
written are entries of a dead tuple, which VACUUM removes, as after a crash
between two indexes of one row today. The cost is one record header and
block reference per key, where one record per leaf would do. A record that
carries several keys for one leaf can be added later (rmgr opcodes 0xE0 and
0xF0 are free), if measurements of documents with many neighboring keys
show it matters; it would need a redo that applies a sequence of per-key
changes.

'''Uniqueness hooks.''' M1 refuses UNIQUE indexes, exclusion constraints
and ON CONFLICT arbiters on an index with an extracted column; M4 allows
them. Three things M1 already does make M4 correct. A row's keys are
distinct after de-duplication, so the check for key ''k'' never finds the
row's own other keys: they are different keys, in other runs. An entry
whose heap TID is the row's own is never a conflict, which
bark_check_unique already applies to UNIQUE_CHECK_EXISTING, where every key
of the row is already in the index. And after a wait on a conflicting
transaction, the insert re-descends for the key it was checking, not the
row's first key: keys already inserted stay inserted, and inserting one
again would be reported as corruption ("Heap TID order" in the README). M4
also needs the check for every key to happen before any key of the row is
inserted, or to tolerate a row whose first keys are in and a later key
conflicts (the error aborts the transaction, so the entries are dead, as
above); that is M4's to decide.

'''CREATE INDEX.''' bark_build_callback does the same extraction, sort and
de-duplication and hands one tuple per key to tuplesort, which orders
(key, TID) as today: tuplesort_begin_index_btree reads procedure 1 and 2
through the column's family and input type, where a multikey class
registers them. Parallel workers extract in their own callbacks. A key too
large for the sort is left for the oversized pass, which runs procedure 7
again on the row (it is immutable) and inserts only those keys. The build
counts (key, TID) members, not rows, as VACUUM does.

==== Scans ====

'''Keys on the extracted column.''' At rescan BARK calls procedure 8 for
each scan key on the extracted column and gets its boundaries. One such key
positions the scan. Its boundaries extend the descent bound the way an
equality prefix and a bound do today (bark_make_bound): when every column
before the extracted one has an equality key, the scan descends to the first
boundary's start, walks the boundaries in order, and re-descends where the
next boundary starts past the page's high key, as the leading-column array
scan does. Otherwise the boundaries are a filter on each entry. An entry
inside a boundary whose extract-query asked for a recheck goes to procedure
9; false drops the entry, maybe returns it with <code>xs_recheck</code>.
NULL entries match only a scan for NULLs, or a scan key whose procedure 8
set <code>searchnulls</code>; such a key reads them as well as its
boundaries, and its rows come back with <code>xs_recheck</code>, since a
NULL entry may be a NULL value, an empty value or a NULL element. A query
whose answer includes the empty value needs it: for <code>int4[]</code>,
<code>&lt;@</code> (the empty array is contained in every array),
<code>@&gt; '{}'</code> (one unbounded boundary, which does not reach the
NULL entries) and <code>= '{}'</code> (no boundaries at all).

If there are several keys on the extracted column, BARK positions on the
first and sets <code>xs_recheck</code> for every row the scan returns. Two
keys on a multikey column mean "some key satisfies the first and some key
satisfies the second", possibly different keys, so their boundaries cannot be
intersected (MongoDB has the same rule for multikey bounds). A class that
means one key satisfying both (MongoDB's <code>$elemMatch</code>) provides
one operator whose extract-query returns the intersection. A ScalarArrayOp
on the extracted column calls procedure 8 once per element and unions the
boundaries. Boundaries that intersect are merged, and a merged boundary is
not passed to procedure 9; its rows are returned with
<code>xs_recheck</code> instead.

'''Returning a row once.''' A plain or index-only scan (amgettuple) returns
a row at its first entry in scan order and drops its later entries. It does
this whenever a row can match through two keys: always, unless the meta
page says the index has never held a multikey row ("Multikey metadata"
below), or the extracted column has exactly one boundary and it is a point.
A bitmap scan never does: TIDBitmap absorbs repeats.

The returned rows are kept in a ''seen set'' per scan: a radix tree
(<code>lib/radixtree.h</code>, local, in the scan's memory context, as
TidStore uses it) whose key is <code>bark_tid_to_key(tid) &gt;&gt; 6</code>
and whose value is a 64-bit word with one bit per TID of that window. The
scan tests and sets a TID's bit when it is about to return the TID. It does
not set the bit for an entry that a filter or procedure 9 rejected, since a
later entry of the row may match. A TID that the heap later finds invisible
or that fails the executor's recheck stays in the set; its other entries
would fail the same way, since visibility and the heap recheck are per row.
The set is not sbm. A scan in key order meets TIDs in random heap order, and
sbm, which is built for ordered bulk loads, walks its chunk list on every
random insert. test_sbm_benchmark (EC2 c7i.4xlarge, assert-enabled build,
random TIDs) measured a random <code>sbm_add_grow</code> at 49 us per TID
for 10,000 TIDs, 66 us for 100,000, and 530-660 us for 100,000-300,000
over a universe of 29.1M. In the same runs a sorted
<code>sbm_add_many_grow</code> took 330-410 ns per TID, TIDBitmap 54-128
ns, Bitmapset 5-22 ns. The gap grows with the set, so it is the algorithm,
not the assertions. TIDBitmap goes lossy past its budget, and a lossy page
cannot answer "seen". TidStore replaces a block's offsets in one call, with
no add of a single TID.

Rows that fall in different groups of the columns before the extracted one
cannot share an entry group: all of a row's entries have the same fixed
columns. So, when the extracted column is not the first column, the set is
cleared each time the scan moves to a new value of those columns, and holds
at most one group's rows.

'''Memory.''' The set costs between one bit and about 16 bytes (a word plus
radix-tree nodes, for TIDs scattered one per window) per row returned since
the last clear. A <code>LIMIT</code> bounds it. Its budget is
<code>get_hash_memory_limit()</code> (work_mem times hash_mem_multiplier),
since it is a hash-like table. barkcostestimate keeps plans inside it: an
amgettuple path that needs the set and whose estimated rows per group,
times 16 bytes, exceed the budget gets <code>disable_cost</code> added, so
the planner takes a bitmap scan and a Sort. At run time the set grows past
the budget rather than return a row twice, as a hashed RecursiveUnion's table
does. Spilling would not help, since every entry must be tested in order.

'''Ordered scans.''' The planner orders an index's rows by its key
columns up to, not including, the extracted column. M1 changes plancat to
give such an index its own <code>sortopfamily</code> array, with InvalidOid at
the extracted column (today it reuses <code>opfamily</code> whenever
<code>ambtreeopfamilies</code> is set), and build_index_pathkeys to stop at an
InvalidOid, as it stops at an INCLUDE column. Rows come back in that order:
all of a row's entries are in one group of the leading columns, and the row
comes back at the first of them. A KNN scan (<code>&lt;~&gt;</code> on a fixed
leading column) keeps the seen set too, without clearing it, since its two
cursors interleave groups.

An order on the extracted column itself is an ordering operator of the
class (<code>amoppurpose</code> 'o', <code>FOR ORDER BY</code> a btree
family of its sort type, as DocumentDB's <code>|&lt;&gt;</code> and
<code>&lt;&gt;|</code> are). BARK serves it on the leading column only, as
it serves KNN (match_pathkeys_to_index). For the ordering key, procedure 8
returns the boundaries to walk (all of the column, or one path's range under
M5) and sets <code>backward</code> in its seventh argument to walk them
backward. An ordered scan with no filter on the column must return every
row, and a row whose value has no keys has only its NULL entry, so the
ordering key also sets <code>searchnulls</code>: the scan reads the NULL
entries at the position the column's NULLS option gives them, and their rows
sort as the operator's NULL result does. The scan walks
them in key order, forward or backward, with the seen set, and returns the
key of each row's first entry as <code>xs_orderbyvals</code>, exact. This is
MongoDB's sort rule for arrays, without a Sort node: an ascending sort sees
a document at its smallest element, a descending one at its largest.

MongoDB's rule has one more part: the sort key ignores the filter. With
<code>{genres: {$gt: "P"}}</code> sorted ascending on <code>genres</code>,
a document sorts by its smallest genre even when that genre does not match.
A scan positioned on the filter's boundaries would see the document first
at its smallest ''matching'' key, a different order. So when a scan has
both an ordering key and a filter on the extracted column, the filter does
not position it. The scan walks the ordering key's boundaries, returns each
row at its first entry with <code>xs_recheck</code> set, and the executor's
heap recheck applies the filter. That reads the whole column range, which
barkcostestimate charges.

'''Mark and restore.''' A merge join restores a scan to a mark and reads
the rows after it again, and the seen set would drop them. bark_markpos
starts an undo list. Every TID the scan adds to the set after the mark is
appended to it, and bark_restrpos clears those TIDs from the set and empties
the list. The list counts against the set's budget.

'''Backward and scroll.''' A scan that starts backward is fine: the seen
set works in either direction. A scan that reverses direction is not. Going
back over rows it returned, it meets their entries, which the set drops, and
it does not know which entry each row was returned at. So an index with an
extracted column does not support backward scans, which a scroll cursor
needs. The executor has to ask that per index, so M1 changes
IndexSupportsBackwardScan (execAmi.c) to consult the AM's
<code>amproperty</code> for AMPROP_BACKWARD_SCAN before
<code>amcanbackward</code>, and BARK's new bark_property answers false for
such an index. The answer depends on the catalog, not on the meta page's
multikey flag, so a plan stays valid. A scroll cursor then gets a Material
node, as it does over a KNN scan.

'''Parallel scans.''' Workers read different leaves, and a row's entries
can be on leaves that different workers read. Rather than share a seen set,
a scan that needs one has a single participant: M1 changes plancat to set
<code>amcanparallel</code> false for an index with an extracted column (a
per-index answer, from the opclasses already loaded there). A
parallel bitmap heap scan still works, since one process builds its bitmap.
Parallel CREATE INDEX is unaffected.

'''Index-only scans.''' The entry holds a key, not the column's value, and
its type is the class's storage type, so an index-only scan cannot return
the extracted column. bark_canreturn, which the planner calls per column
(index_can_return in plancat), answers false for it. A key does not
determine its row's value: a row with several keys has an entry per key,
and the NULL key stands for a NULL value, an empty value and a NULL
element alike, so a value rebuilt from one entry could be a wrong value,
which BARK never returns. (The design once let procedure 14 rebuild the
value; M1c found the NULL key ambiguous, and the lead dropped it: MongoDB
does not cover queries on a multikey path either.) Fixed columns are
returned as now, with the seen set.

'''As built (M1c).''' The scan follows this section, with these choices the
section left open or that differ from it:

* The keys on the extracted column are moved after the scan's other keys at
  rescan, so the btree-style key code (bounds, skip scan, the page-satisfied
  test) never reads them; only the boundaries do.
* Boundaries are kept in index order: on a DESC column procedure 8's upper
  element is a boundary's start. A procedure 8 declared with six arguments
  is called with six, so <code>PG_NARGS()</code> tells a C function shared
  by both declarations whether it has the flags.
* <code>IS NULL</code> on the extracted column reads the NULL entries, and
  <code>IS NOT NULL</code> every key and the NULL entries, both with
  <code>xs_recheck</code>: a NULL entry may stand for an empty value.
* Procedure 9 runs before the seen set, so a rejected entry never enters
  it. A scan with no procedure 9 whose procedure 8 asks for a recheck is
  an error, as is a key on the column of a class without procedure 8.
* The boundaries are the last required key of A2's odometer
  (<code>BarkArrayKeyState.mk</code>), so they position, re-descend and end
  the scan with the code that walks equality arrays; no third cursor kind.
* The seen set is filled as a page is read, so a page's copied items are
  free of repeats; a mark on that page restores to them unchanged, and the
  undo list covers the pages read after a mark. While a mark is set, the
  set is not emptied for a new group of the fixed columns (a merge join
  re-reads across groups), so it can then hold more than one group's rows.
* The single-point shortcut applies under an MVCC snapshot only; any other
  snapshot always uses the set, as the card for M1c asked.
* Until M1d gives the planner per-index answers, the scan refuses rather
  than answers wrong: a change of direction with the set in use, a
  parallel scan that needs the set, and an ordering key on the extracted
  column raise errors. bark_canreturn answers false for the extracted
  column (an index-only scan would otherwise return keys as values).

'''As built (M1d, in progress).''' Done: plancat sets
<code>amcanparallel</code> false for an index with an extracted column (the
same test that puts InvalidOid in its <code>sortopfamily</code>), so the
planner never makes a parallel scan that needs the seen set; IndexSupportsBackwardScan asks
<code>amproperty</code> (AMPROP_BACKWARD_SCAN, attno 0) and falls back to
<code>amcanbackward</code> when the AM has none or does not answer, and
bark_property answers backward_scan from the operator classes (it opens
the index for bark_index_extracted_column), orderable, asc, desc,
nulls_first and nulls_last false for the extracted column and every key
column after it (they form no pathkeys; the generic code answers for the
others and for INCLUDE columns), and distance_orderable for the leading
column as SP-GiST does (an ordering operator whose result its sort family
can sort). A cursor not declared SCROLL is not made scrollable over such
an index, as over GIN. The scan's errors for a direction change and for a
parallel scan with the set in use stay, for callers of the AM that do not
ask the planner's questions. Not yet: the ordering operator on the
extracted column, and the costing.
* A DEBUG1 message at scan start says whether the scan uses a seen set, for
  tests.

==== Multikey metadata ====

'''The index-wide flag.''' BarkMetaPageData gains <code>uint32
bark_flags</code> after <code>bark_allequalimage</code>, with
BARK_META_MULTIKEY (bit 0) meaning "this index has held a row with more
than one data entry". An index built before M1 reads 0 there (PageInit
zeroes the page), which is right, since it has no extracted column. So the
format version stays 2. The other 31 bits are spare.

The flag is set before the first entry of the row that needs it, with no
other page locked: the meta page is locked exclusive, the bit set, and the
page logged whole with <code>log_newpage_buffer</code>. That happens once in
an index's life, so it needs no record type of its own. CREATE INDEX sets
it when any row had two or more keys. It is never cleared: VACUUM leaves
it, and only REINDEX, which rebuilds from the rows that exist, can produce
an index without it. MongoDB has the same rule: once multikey, always
multikey until a rebuild. A scan reads the flag at rescan, under the meta
page's share lock, unless a cached copy in <code>rd_amcache</code> already
says multikey (it never changes back, so a cached true is never stale).
Order makes this safe. A row visible to the scan's snapshot committed
before the snapshot was taken, its insert set the flag before that, and the
scan reads the flag after taking its snapshot. A relcache invalidation would
not be safe: it is sent after the commit becomes visible. A scan with a
non-MVCC snapshot has no such order, so it uses the seen set whenever the
index has an extracted column, whatever the flag says.

'''Markers.''' Procedure 7 may also return ''marker keys'', facts about the
index that the class wants recorded once, not per row: "path a.b has held
an array", say. The class gives them keys that no data key can equal
(DocumentDB already makes such terms: a reserved path, flagged as metadata,
with 33 reserved kinds for composite paths). BARK stores a marker as a
SINGLE entry of that key whose heap TID is <code>(InvalidBlockNumber,
1)</code>. No heap block has that number (MaxBlockNumber is one less), so
the TID names no row, and under H1 it sorts after every real TID of the key.
That is the whole format: a marker is an ordinary entry, so markers for
paths, kinds or anything else that M5 or a class needs later take no format
change.

Before inserting a row's keys, BARK inserts each of its markers that is
not in the index yet, never coalesced, by the normal path. Markers are
never removed, so a backend caches the markers it has seen present in
<code>rd_amcache</code>, and a row whose markers are all cached costs
nothing. CREATE INDEX writes each distinct marker once (tuplesort refuses two
tuples with one heap TID, so parallel participants spool their copies with
TIDs of their own on block InvalidBlockNumber, and the load writes the
reserved one). A marker is a SINGLE entry, so a marker key too large for a
page is refused. Every reader skips
an entry with the reserved TID: bark_readpage before matching, barkbulkdelete
before its callback, bottom-up deletion before offering TIDs to the table
AM, the later K2 kill hints, and amcheck, which accepts the reserved TID
only on a SINGLE entry of a column with markers. The class reads a marker
through <code>bark_index_has_marker(Relation, Datum key)</code>, one descent
to (key, reserved TID); a planner support function of the class can call it
to choose a plan for a path that has never held an array. Markers are
monotone and outside MVCC, like the flag, and are inserted before the row's
keys for the same reason.

==== VACUUM, deletion and statistics ====

'''VACUUM.''' Unchanged, and correct as it is. barkbulkdelete visits every
leaf entry and calls the callback for each of its TIDs, so a dead row
loses its entry under every one of its keys, each on its own leaf, in that
leaf's XLOG_BARK_VACUUM record. Nothing there assumes one entry per row.
The POSTING removal reserve, cycle IDs and page deletion are per entry and
per page. <code>tuples_removed</code> counts entries, not rows.

'''Bottom-up deletion (D1).''' Unchanged, and correct as it is. The
executor's <code>indexUnchanged</code> is per index, so it holds for every
key of the new version, and each key's insert may run bark_bottomup_delete
on its own leaf. That pass deletes the entries on that leaf of versions the
table AM reports dead; the same versions' entries under other keys, on
other leaves, stay until VACUUM or their own leaf's pass. An index may
always hold entries of dead rows, so this is not an error.

'''Retail delete (C-DELETE).''' Delete-marking a row runs procedure 7 on
the old value (it is immutable, so it returns the same keys) and marks
(key, TID) for each key. H1 makes each of those one entry member. Markers
are not touched.

'''Statistics.''' The cost model needs keys per row, ''K''. The meta page
gains <code>uint64 bark_nkeys</code>, the number of (key, TID) members
without markers. CREATE INDEX stores the count it inserted, and VACUUM, which
already visits every member (its cleanup-only scan too), stores the count
it leaves (logged like the flag, once per VACUUM). barkcostestimate reads the meta page, as
gincostestimate does, but only for an index with an extracted column, and
takes ''K'' as <code>bark_nkeys</code> over the heap's reltuples, or 1 if the
flag is clear. Between VACUUMs, ''K'' is as old as the last one.

ANALYZE needs nothing from the operator class in M1. It gathers the
column's statistics with the column type's <code>typanalyze</code> (for
arrays, array_typanalyze's MCELEM and DECHIST), and a qual's selectivity
comes from its operator's restriction estimator, as for any index (for
arrays, arraycontsel). A class should give its operators estimators that
understand its semantics; without one the planner assumes 0.5. Statistics
over keys, rather than values, are not part of M1.

'''Costing.''' barkcostestimate counts the entries a scan visits as the
qual's selectivity times reltuples times ''K'', capped at the index's
entries, with one descent per boundary that is not on the page before it,
as the array scan is costed. It adds <code>cpu_operator_cost</code> per
entry for the seen set and for procedure 9 when the boundaries ask for a
recheck. It adds the work_mem penalty above, and for an ordered scan with a
filter on the extracted column, the whole ordering range and a heap fetch
per row. The index correlation is 0 when the extracted column is first. The
rows the scan returns are the qual's selectivity times reltuples, without
''K'': the scan returns each row once.

==== Wildcard indexes as the check ====

M5's wildcard index is the hardest case M1 has to carry, so it is the check
on this design. Its procedure 7 walks a document and emits one key
(path, value) for every path that the class's include/exclude projection
keeps, and one per element under a path that holds an array. The
projection is a procedure 5 option of the column. Its procedure 8 turns
<code>a.b &gt; 5</code> into boundaries inside path <code>a.b</code>:
<code>((a.b, 5), (a.b, +inf))</code> in the class's (path, value) order.
What that needs from M1, and where M1 provides it:

* '''Compound keys (fixed columns, path, value).''' The extracted column
  may follow fixed columns, so <code>bark(tenant, doc wildcard_ops)</code>
  keeps (tenant, path, value), and the seen set is cleared per tenant. The
  path and value are one key of the class's storage type; BARK never splits
  them. Suffix truncation and prefix compression (on the first column) work
  on that key as on any other.
* '''Ranges within a path''' are boundaries; the scan walks them and
  re-descends between them. Skip scan over a leading fixed column works as
  today.
* '''Per-path multikey markers''' are marker keys (path, "has held an
  array"). A query on a path without its marker is known to match each row
  at most once, and a class can then plan an index-only scan or an ordered
  scan with no Sort. Telling the AM "this scan needs no seen set" is an M5
  extension of procedure 8. M1 stores, caches and reads the markers.
* '''Write volume.''' A document with ''N'' projected paths is ''N''
  entries, inserted in one pass. Adjacent paths of one document usually
  land on one leaf, which is where a per-leaf WAL record would pay; M1
  leaves that to measurement.
* '''Sorting by a path''' is an ordering operator whose procedure 8 returns
  the path's range, so MongoDB's array sort rule holds per path, the filter
  rule as above.

Nothing in this list changes the on-disk format.

==== What M1 does not do ====

* Unique indexes, exclusion constraints and ON CONFLICT on an extracted
  column (M4). M1 refuses them.
* GIN's query interface in scans (procedures 10-13; M2). M1 validates them.
* Per-locator data (RUM's addInfo). M3 is closed: a composite key serves
  it.
* Wildcard and path-projection operator classes (M5). M1 provides markers,
  boundaries and compound keys for them.
* More than one extracted column in one index.
* An index-only scan of the extracted column; a backward or scroll scan, or
  a parallel amgettuple scan, on such an index.
* A WAL record for several keys of a row on one leaf.
* Clearing the multikey flag or a marker without REINDEX.
* Key statistics in ANALYZE, and any operator class semantics: type order,
  null versus missing, and the meaning of a NULL key are the class's.
* Any new operator class in core. M1's tests use a test-module class over
  <code>int4[]</code> (elements as keys, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>,
  <code>@&gt;</code> and an element-order operator) and compare its results
  with GIN's <code>array_ops</code> and with a sequential scan on the same
  data.

=== M4: unique indexes over multikey keys ===

The design of record for M4; the implementation card follows from it. It
builds on M1's terms (extracted column, fixed column, NULL key, marker) and
on its "Uniqueness hooks".

==== What is unique ====

A unique index with an extracted column is unique over its entries' key
columns: no two rows have an entry with equal key columns, that is, equal
fixed columns and an equal key. Every row's keys are de-duplicated ("Keys of
a row"), so a row may repeat a key in its value, and two rows conflict when
they share one key under equal fixed columns. This is MongoDB's documented
rule for unique multikey indexes: the constraint applies across separate
documents, and one document's array may repeat a value. A compound index
<code>bark(tenant, tags) UNIQUE</code> says that no two rows of one tenant
share a tag; MongoDB's compound unique index over a scalar field and an
array field means the same.

'''NULLs.''' NULLs are distinct, as PostgreSQL's default for unique indexes
has them, but per key: a key with a NULL in any key column is never checked
and never conflicts. A NULL fixed column puts a NULL in every key of the
row, so such a row is not checked at all, as today. A NULL value, an empty
value or a NULL element is the NULL key, so it never conflicts, while the
same row's other keys are still checked. So a unique index with an
extracted column does not make the column's values unique: any number of
rows may hold an empty array.

'''NULLS NOT DISTINCT is refused''' on an index with an extracted column
(bark_check_multikey_index). The NULL key stands for three things ("Keys
of a row"), so under it a row with an empty array would conflict with a row
with a NULL value or with <code>'{7,NULL}'</code>, which no user means. The
executor's pre-check ("ON CONFLICT and the executor's pre-check" below) also
could not find such a conflict: it scans with the column's conflict
operator, and no operator over two values says "both have the NULL key".
An insert that the pre-check passes and the index rejects is retried by
ExecInsert without end. NULLS NOT DISTINCT over the fixed columns alone
would be well defined. It is left out because PostgreSQL has a single
per-index option.

'''MongoDB's null and missing rules are the operator class's.''' MongoDB
indexes a missing field and an explicit null as the key null, so a unique
index accepts only one document that lacks the field. A null array element
also indexes as null. An empty array indexes as undefined, so two documents
with <code>a: []</code> conflict too; that is this note's reading of the
documentation, not checked on a server. A sparse unique index skips
the documents that lack the field, and a partial one skips those its filter
rejects. DocumentDB's class emits those keys as ordinary, non-NULL BSON
values (BSON null, undefined), so they conflict under BARK's default NULL
rule, and BARK's NULL key never arises from a document. That is where
pg_extended_btree's issue #17 puts the work too: "uniqueness constraints,
specifically handling MongoDB's approach to null and missing fields", in the
comparison and term logic of the class. MongoDB's sparse and partial unique
indexes are partial indexes (<code>WHERE</code>), which BARK has. If
DocumentDB expresses sparse in its term generation instead, by emitting no
term for a missing path, that is a class matter as well; this note has not
checked which it does.

==== When the check runs ====

'''Each key is checked as it is inserted.''' bark_insert_entries checks a
row's keys one at a time, in the row's ascending key order, each under the
exclusive lock of the leaf where the key's run starts, and inserts the key
before releasing that lock. That is nbtree's protocol for one key
(_bt_doinsert, _bt_check_unique, _bt_findinsertloc) applied N times: the
check and the insert of a key are atomic with respect to every other
inserter of that key, which must check from the same leaf. When key
''k'' conflicts with a committed row the insert raises the unique violation
as today. Keys 0..''k''-1 are already in the index, and the error aborts
the transaction (or subtransaction) that inserted the heap tuple, so they
are entries of a dead tuple, which VACUUM removes. A row that fails on its
second unique index today leaves its first index's entry the same way.

The alternative, checking all N keys before inserting any, is rejected.
Without keeping every leaf locked from check to insert, another backend can
insert a key between the two, so each key has to be checked again when it
is inserted, and the first pass only makes the failing case cheaper: 2N
leaf visits for every row that succeeds, to spare VACUUM ''k'' entries of
one that fails. Keeping the leaves locked instead would hold up to N
exclusive leaf locks at once, blocking every reader of those leaves for the
whole row, and a document with a few hundred keys would exceed the
per-backend limit on held LWLocks (MAX_SIMUL_LWLOCKS, 200, in lwlock.c).

'''Cost.''' A row of N keys costs what M1's one pass costs, plus the
per-key check: a search for the start of the key's run on the leaf the key
goes on, and a heap fetch under SnapshotDirty for each entry of the key
that is there (in a unique index, usually none, or the dead versions of
one row). The descent is still shared. The one-pass rule changes in one
place: a unique check must start on the first leaf that can hold the key,
so, for a unique index, key ''i''+1 stays on L, or steps to R, by
bark_search's move-right test for a unique check (the key alone, no heap
TID), and then moves right to its heap TID's position with
bark_insert_stepright as a single-key insert does. L is then the first
leaf that can hold key ''i''+1, since key ''i'' is on L and sorts before
it. M1's <code>Assert(nitups == 1 || !checkingunique)</code> in
bark_insert_entries goes, and so does the Assert in bark_insert that a row
with an extracted column has no unique check. A unique index never
coalesces (bark_insert_state), with or without an extracted column, so
every entry in it is SINGLE or OVERSIZED, as bark_check_unique expects.

'''Waits.''' When key ''k'' conflicts with a row whose inserter or deleter
is in progress, bark_insert_entries releases its leaf, waits
(XactLockTableWait, or SpeculativeInsertionWait for a speculative
inserter), and descends again for key ''k'', as M1's "Uniqueness hooks"
require; keys 0..''k''-1 stay inserted. It waits holding no buffer lock,
but with its own earlier keys in the index, where other inserters may
wait on them. Waits on inserters cannot form a cycle between two rows. Say
T1 waits on T2's entry of key ''a'' while T2 waits on T1's entry of key
''b''. T1 inserted ''b'' before reaching ''a'', so ''b'' &lt; ''a''; T2
inserted ''a'' before reaching ''b'', so ''a'' &lt; ''b''. Both rows insert
in ascending key order under one opclass comparator, so this cannot hold.
Waits on an in-progress deleter can form a cycle, as they can in nbtree
with one key per row today (two UPDATEs that swap their keys). Multikey
adds one shape: T1 waits on T2's delete of an old row that has key ''a'',
while T2's new row waits on T1's entry of key ''b'', which T1 inserted
first. T1's row and T2's new row share ''b'', so one of them was bound to
fail; the deadlock detector aborts one, which is the outcome
execIndexing.c's header accepts for exclusion constraints. A transaction
that inserts two rows can deadlock with another as it can in nbtree.

'''UNIQUE_CHECK_PARTIAL''' (ON CONFLICT's speculative insertion, and
logical replication's conflict detection, which ExecInsertIndexTuples
gives EIIT_NO_DUPE_ERROR) never waits and never errors: bark_check_unique
returns at the first conflict, as _bt_check_unique does. The row's result
is false if any key conflicted. Every key is still inserted, as nbtree
inserts the entry, and once one key has conflicted the remaining keys are
inserted without a check, since the result cannot change. The executor
then kills the speculative tuple (table_tuple_complete_speculative), which
makes all N entries dead, and retries from its pre-check, which is where
an ON CONFLICT insert waits (next section). The AM never waits under
PARTIAL, so the speculative path adds no wait between rows at all.

==== ON CONFLICT and the executor's pre-check ====

The executor finds conflicts without the AM as well. ExecInsert's
pre-check, and logical replication's FindConflictTuple, call
ExecCheckIndexConstraints, which scans the index under a dirty snapshot
with one key per index column (check_exclusion_or_unique_constraint). The
operators come from BuildSpeculativeIndexInfo (index.c), which asks the AM
for each column's COMPARE_EQ strategy and errors when there is none.
ExecOpenIndices calls it for ''every'' unique index of a table that an
INSERT ... ON CONFLICT targets, arbiter or not, so today an ON CONFLICT
insert into any table with a unique multikey index would fail there.

'''The conflict operator.''' Two rows conflict on an extracted column when
their values share a key: that is overlap, not equality. M4 adds
procedure '''15''', translate-cmptype, <code>int2 (int4)</code>: GiST's
procedure 12 in purpose and signature. For a BARK family,
bark_translate_cmptype passes only COMPARE_OVERLAP to it and returns its
strategy; every other compare type stays InvalidStrategy, and
bark_translate_strategy stays COMPARE_INVALID, so the planner still reads no
equality into a multikey operator, and a foreign key or a merge join still
finds none. BuildSpeculativeIndexInfo, when a column has no COMPARE_EQ
strategy, asks for COMPARE_OVERLAP before it errors. That is the only core
change the pre-check needs. The operator is looked up with
<code>get_opfamily_member(opfamily, opcintype, opcintype, strategy)</code>,
as the equality is, so the class registers it under its input type.
Core's <code>&amp;&amp;</code> is declared over <code>anyarray</code>, so
the test class adds an overlap operator over <code>int4[]</code> under a
name of its own. A new name keeps core's operator resolving as before, so
the GIN comparisons in the test still plan. <code>amsupport</code> becomes
15; barkvalidate checks the signature, barkadjustmembers makes it a soft
dependency as gistadjustmembers does GiST's 12, and refuses it in a btree
family as it refuses 7-14. bark_check_multikey_index refuses a unique index
on an extracted column whose class does not translate COMPARE_OVERLAP.
The strategy must be one that the class's procedure 8 accepts.

'''The operator must agree with the keys.''' The class's overlap operator
must be true exactly when the two values share a key that is not NULL. If
it says false where the AM finds a conflict, ExecInsert retries without end:
the pre-check passes, the index reports a speculative conflict, and the
retry repeats both. If it says true where the AM finds none, ON CONFLICT
fires for a row that could have been inserted. For arrays,
<code>&amp;&amp;</code> qualifies: it ignores NULL elements, and an empty
array overlaps nothing.

'''The pre-check scan''' is an ordinary BARK scan: equality keys on the
fixed columns and the overlap key on the extracted column, whose procedure
8 gives one point boundary per key of the new value. It runs under a dirty
snapshot, so it keeps a seen set ("Multikey metadata"), and returns each
row once. That matters here: with a valid tupleid,
check_exclusion_or_unique_constraint raises "found self tuple multiple
times" for a row it sees twice. When the scan sets <code>xs_recheck</code>,
index_recheck_constraint calls the overlap operator on the two values,
which is exact. NULLs agree with the AM: under NULLS DISTINCT the pre-check
passes a row with any NULL column, and such a row has only NULL keys or
NULL fixed columns.

'''One row may conflict with several.''' A new row <code>{1,2}</code>
conflicts with a row <code>{1}</code> and a row <code>{2}</code>. The
pre-check returns the first conflicting row it meets, which is the one
holding the new value's smallest shared key. ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE updates
that row only, and DO NOTHING inserts nothing. A single btree arbiter
cannot produce this case, but PostgreSQL already accepts it for several
arbiter indexes: one row per proposed row is affected. Logical
replication reports such a row as one insert_exists conflict.

==== Deferred checks, and UPDATE ====

'''UNIQUE_CHECK_EXISTING''' is what unique_key_recheck (constraint.c)
passes to recheck a deferred constraint. Only a constraint can be
<code>DEFERRABLE</code>, and no constraint can use a BARK index until U1
(parse_utilcmd.c refuses one "not a btree"), so it is unreachable in M4, as
it is for scalar BARK indexes today; M4 only makes it right for when U1
lands. Under it bark_insert extracts the row's keys and checks each, by
the own-TID rule bark_check_unique already applies (M1's "Uniqueness
hooks"), inserting nothing. Each key's check must also find the row's own
entry, and raises nbtree's "failed to re-find tuple within index" if it
does not, as _bt_check_unique does: procedure 7 is immutable, so a missing
key means a broken class. A deferred row inserted under
UNIQUE_CHECK_PARTIAL has every key in the index ("When the check runs"),
so the recheck sees all of them.

'''UPDATE of a row whose keys change partly''', from
<code>{1,2,3}</code> to <code>{2,3,4}</code>, is a new heap version with a
new TID (the column changed, so it is not HOT), and its keys 2 and 3 meet
the old version's entries. Those are not conflicts. The updating
transaction is the old version's deleter, and HeapTupleSatisfiesDirty
treats a tuple deleted by the current transaction as invisible, so
bark_check_unique's fetch under SnapshotDirty skips it. That is why a
non-HOT UPDATE that keeps a btree unique key does not conflict with itself
today; the own-TID rule is not involved, since the TIDs differ. Another
transaction inserting key 2 meanwhile finds the old version with an
in-progress deleter and waits for the update to finish, then finds the new
version. An UPDATE that changes only another column of a non-HOT row
re-inserts all of the row's keys (indexUnchanged), and each one meets the
old version's entry of the same key, skipped the same way.

'''Error detail.''' BuildIndexValueDescription (genam.c) prints each column
with the output function of the opclass's input type, so it must never be
given a key of the storage type: <code>array_out</code> on an
<code>int4</code> key would crash. bark_check_unique passes it the row's
values as bark_insert received them, and, when it returns a description (it
returns NULL when the user may not see the columns, or under row security),
adds the shared key, printed with the storage type's output function:
<code>Key (tenant, tags)=(1, {1,2,3}) shares key 2
with an existing row.</code> MongoDB's E11000 message also names the
duplicate key, not the document. bark_load deforms a sorted key tuple and
passes it to BuildIndexValueDescription, which for an extracted column is
the same mistake; there it gives only <code>Duplicate keys exist.</code>,
nbtree's detail when no description is available, and an insert of either
row names the key.

==== CREATE INDEX ====

bark_load already raises the unique violation when a sorted tuple's key
columns equal its predecessor's and have no NULL. With an extracted
column, the sort holds one tuple per (key, TID), a row's keys are distinct,
so two equal adjacent tuples come from two rows: the check is right for
multikey as it stands. Markers are skipped before it, as they are now, and
insert puts them in unchecked (bark_insert_markers); a marker's key equals
no data key, so it can conflict with nothing. What the build must add is
the treatment of dead rows.

'''Dead rows are indexed and not checked.''' Before card B12,
bark_build_callback dropped a row that table_index_build_scan reports with
<code>tupleIsAlive</code> false, a RECENTLY_DEAD row among them, which
nbtree indexes because an older snapshot may still read it through the
index. B12 fixed that as nbtree does with its second spool
(_bt_build_callback, and _bt_load, which merges it): in a unique index,
dead rows go to a second sort that is never unique-checked, and bark_load
compares a live tuple only with the previous ''live'' tuple. A multikey
index cannot be unique in M1, so bark_build_row_keys still puts every key
in the main sort. M4 takes that from B12 and adds to it: a dead row's keys
go to the second sort. A
RECENTLY_DEAD row that shares a key with a live row, which is what an
UPDATE that keeps a key leaves, is then not a violation. A dead row's
oversized keys go to the oversized pass with UNIQUE_CHECK_NO, and a live
row's with UNIQUE_CHECK_YES. bark_insert_oversized_keys, which passes
UNIQUE_CHECK_NO for every row in M1b, takes the check from its caller.
The live keys they meet are already loaded, and the dead ones are
invisible to bark_check_unique's dirty snapshot. Parallel workers spool
their dead rows' keys to the second sort the same way.

CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY's validate_index inserts the rows the first scan
missed through bark_insert with UNIQUE_CHECK_YES
(heapam_index_validate_scan), so they are checked key by key as any insert
is.

==== Scans, the planner and amcheck ====

'''Scans are unchanged.''' A unique index with an extracted column is read
as M1's index is read: the seen set, <code>xs_recheck</code> wherever M1
sets it, the same cost model. Uniqueness gives a scan nothing to drop:
every rule in M1's "Scans" that sets <code>xs_recheck</code> (a NULL entry,
two keys on the extracted column, procedure 9's maybe, a filter under an
ordering key) is about which rows match the qual, not about how many rows
hold a key, so it applies as before. The one fact uniqueness adds, that a
point boundary on the extracted column under equalities on every fixed
column finds at most one live row, is not used to stop the scan early;
M1's seen set already returns that row once.

'''Core must not read the index as a unique key.''' Core takes an index
with <code>indisunique</code> and an equality clause on every key column as
proof that at most one row matches. relation_has_unique_index_for
(indxpath.c) and the GROUP BY reduction in remove_useless_groupby_columns
(initsplan.c) check the clause's operator against the column's opfamily,
and a BARK family has no equality (bark_translate_strategy), so they never
match the extracted column. has_unique_index (plancat.c), which marks a
column's statistics <code>isunique</code> for the equality-selectivity
estimators, and barkcostestimate's <code>numIndexTuples = 1.0</code> look
only at the column number and the clause positions, not the operator. Both
would be wrong here: a row may hold many keys, and the column's values are
not unique (two rows may both hold the empty array). M4 makes
get_relation_info set <code>info->unique</code> false for an index with an
extracted column, in the loop where M1 already gives such a column
InvalidOid in <code>sortopfamily</code>; every planner reader above,
barkcostestimate included, reads that field. infer_arbiter_indexes reads
<code>pg_index</code> directly, so ON CONFLICT inference still finds the
index.

Two commands pick a unique index from <code>pg_index</code> and only then
look for its columns' equality operators, and so would fail on this one
rather than pass it by. transformFkeyCheckAttrs (tablecmds.c) takes the
first valid unique index over the referenced columns, constraint or not,
and ATAddForeignKeyConstraint then raises "could not identify an equality
operator for foreign key", even when a btree unique index on the same
columns comes later in the list. REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY
(matview.c's is_usable_unique_index, then refresh_by_match_merge) raises an
internal "missing equality operator" error the same way. M4 makes both
skip an index any of whose key columns has no COMPARE_EQ strategy
(IndexAmTranslateCompareType), so a foreign key cannot reference the
extracted column, and a btree index beside it is still found. REPLICA
IDENTITY USING INDEX (ATExecReplicaIdentity) refuses such an index with an
error saying why, since replication's lookup by identity
(build_replindex_scan_key) needs equality on every column.

'''amcheck.''' bark_index_check and bark_index_parent_check gain
<code>checkunique</code> (amcheck 1.8), as bt_index_check and
bt_index_parent_check have it, for every unique BARK index. It walks each run of equal key columns, which may
cross leaves, and reports index corruption when two entries of the run
point to heap tuples visible to the check's MVCC snapshot, as
bt_entry_unique_check does. The run is of (fixed columns, key), so for an
extracted column that is exactly M4's rule. One row's entries are never in
one run, since its keys are distinct, so the check needs no own-row
exception. A run whose key has a NULL column is skipped, as nbtree skips
keys with <code>anynullkeys</code>. Markers are skipped, as every reader
skips them; a marker's key equals no data key, so it is alone in its run.
heapallindexed is refused on an index with an extracted
column (bark_check_structure, M1b), unique or not; making it form a row's keys
through procedure 7 is separate work, which uniqueness does not change.

==== What M4 does not do ====

* NULLS NOT DISTINCT on an index with an extracted column, or over its
  fixed columns alone.
* Exclusion constraints on an extracted column (the conflict operator would
  serve them, but they come with U1's constraint surface).
* Deferrable uniqueness and primary keys on the index (U1), and foreign
  keys that reference an extracted column, which need an equality.
* A unique wildcard index (M5): it is a unique index whose key is (path,
  value), so M4 provides it, but its semantics, unique per path, are M5's
  to state and test.
* Any MongoDB value semantics: null, missing, undefined, type order and
  sparseness are the class's.
* A check of all of a row's keys before any is inserted.
* Tests are the implementation card's: M4's rules, each against a unique
  btree index over an expression that gives one key per row where it can
  (<code>bark(a)</code> with one-element arrays against btree on
  <code>a[1]</code>), and against a sequential scan elsewhere: a row that
  repeats a key; two rows sharing one key; compound keys; NULL and empty
  values; ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING and DO UPDATE with one and two conflicting
  rows; an UPDATE that keeps some keys; a build over RECENTLY_DEAD rows;
  concurrent inserters sharing keys (isolation spec, including the
  deleter-wait deadlock); amcheck's checkunique on a corrupted index.

=== M3: per-locator data (closed) ===

'''Decision: M3 is not needed and is closed.''' An entry whose members
carry a value and are ordered by it is in the same order as a composite
key (key, value), and holds the same members. Its one gain is size, a
little over half a composite key's per member for an 8-byte value. In return every
reader that treats an entry as one key would have to learn otherwise, and
every insert would rewrite an entry. Each use case below is served by a
composite key. The limit they share is a scan and planner limit, ordering
past the extracted column, which M3 would have had too. It goes to M2.

==== What M3 would be ====

A LIST variant, <code>(key, [(value, TID), ...])</code> with members
ordered by (value, TID). No POSTING variant is possible: an sbm is a set of
TIDs, and its only order is TID order. Values kept beside it in a parallel
array cost at least the value per member and are not in value order. Under
H1 a composite key <code>bark(k, v)</code> orders its entries by (k, v, TID),
so an M3 entry is a run of composite-key entries with the key stored once.
Any scan over one is the same scan over the other.

What that costs, beyond writing the shape:

* An entry would hold members that differ in a key column. The scan tests
  a qual once per entry (bark_readpage), the split cuts an entry by TID
  (bark_entry_cut, bark_singleval_cut), amcheck and H1 require the entries
  of one key to have disjoint, ascending TID ranges (the README's
  "Invariants"), and a pivot can only fall between entries. Each of these
  would have to treat an M3 entry as a sequence of keys.
* Every insert would rewrite a LIST entry through the overwrite path. The
  insert measurements under "Value sets that outgrow one entry" give that
  path's cost: 10M scattered rows took 11593 MB of WAL as LIST appends and
  1672 MB as SINGLE entries (ks, run 5). Those were appends. In value
  order a member lands inside the entry whenever values do not arrive
  ascending, and a full entry must then be cut. A timestamp mostly
  ascends; a score does not.
* A composite key inserts a SINGLE, the cheap path, or coalesces into an
  entry of equal (k, v) as any BARK key does. Distinct values mean
  SINGLE entries.

==== Worked examples ====

Sizes are computed from the formats, with an 8-kB page, and are not
measured. A SINGLE entry is an 8-byte IndexTupleData header plus its key
columns, MAXALIGNed, plus a 4-byte line pointer.

'''Term search ordered by a timestamp''' (RUM's
<code>rum_tsvector_addon_ops</code> with <code>WITH (attach = 'ts', to =
'body')</code>, queried with <code>ORDER BY ts &lt;=| now()</code>; the
operator names are from RUM's README as this note recalls it). In BARK it is
<code>bark(tags bark_int4_array_ops, ts)</code>: one entry (tag, ts, TID)
per tag of a row. For <code>WHERE tags @&gt; '{7}' ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT
10</code>, tag 7's run is in ts order, and the extracted column has one
point boundary, so no row repeats and no seen set is needed (M1's
"Returning a row once"). A walk backward from the end of the run could stop
after ten rows; the scan rule that lets the planner ask for it is the third
example's, and it is the same rule for M3's entry.
An entry is 8 + 4 + 8, with ts aligned, which is 24 bytes, plus 4: 28 bytes
per (tag, row). A normalized table of (tag, ts) rows under nbtree takes
the same 28, since deduplication needs equal (tag, ts). M3 would take a 6-byte TID and an
8-byte value, 14 to 16 bytes per member, plus about 20 bytes per entry of
up to about 170 members. RUM's own format stores a compressed TID and the
value, so it should be near M3's size; this note has not measured it.
The plain alternative, <code>bark(tags)</code> with LIST and POSTING
entries and a Sort, costs at most 6 bytes per member, but it fetches every
matching row from the heap before the first row comes back. With a
varlena key, a DocumentDB term say, leaf prefix compression stores only
the bytes a key does not share with its page's prefix, so a long key that
repeats on a page costs little more than an int4 one.

Two terms, <code>@&gt; '{7,9}'</code>, are two point boundaries, and M1
returns their rows in no order. But ts is a column of the row, so both runs
are in (ts, TID) order and a row has the same (ts, TID) in each. Their
intersection is a merge in that order, which can stop at the LIMIT: the
leapfrog of "Containment queries", over (ts, TID) instead of TID. It is a
later scan feature, and it works the same with a composite key or with M3.

'''Ranking by a per-key score''' (a weight per term and document). The
score differs between a row's keys, so it cannot be a fixed column. It
is part of the class's key, as M5's path is: a storage type (term, score),
ordered by term, then score descending, with one key per distinct term from
procedure 7. For an int4 term and a float4 score packed into an 8-byte
pass-by-value key, an entry is 16 bytes plus 4: 20 per member, against 10
for M3 with a 4-byte value. "By the score of term t" is an ordering
operator of the class whose procedure 8 returns t's range. M1's ordered
scan walks that range, so rows come back in score order, each once, with the
key as <code>xs_orderbyvals</code>. A rank over several terms (a sum,
BM25) needs neither design. It is the block-max work that "Containment
queries" leaves to a text-search index. So does RUM's tsvector ranking,
whose positions are variable-length data per occurrence, which M3's "small
value" would not have held either.

'''ORDER BY a column after the extracted one.''' MongoDB's compound index
<code>{tags: 1, created: -1}</code> with <code>find({tags: "x"}).sort({created:
-1})</code>: MongoDB's documented rule lets an index provide a sort on the
fields after an equality prefix. This note takes that rule to hold when the
equality field is multikey and the sort field is not an array, and has not
checked it on a server. In BARK the index is <code>bark(tags ops, created
DESC)</code>, and the entries for tag x are in created order, which is the
order the query wants. M1 cannot produce the plan. build_index_pathkeys
stops at the extracted column (InvalidOid in <code>sortopfamily</code>,
M1's "Ordered scans"), and nothing makes that column redundant, as an
equivalence class with a constant makes <code>a</code> redundant under
<code>a = 5</code> for btree(a, b). So <code>ORDER BY created</code> never
matches the index. M3 would not have changed that: its entry for tag x
would be in the same order, behind the same pathkeys.

So M2 needs a scan rule, which M3 would have needed too: an ordering
operator on a fixed column after the extracted one, KNN as BARK serves it
on a leading column today, when every earlier column has an equality and
the extracted column has one point boundary. The KNN scan's two cursors
then run inside that point's run, and no row repeats in it.
match_pathkeys_to_index offers an ordered index's ordering operator on its
leading column only, since a later column's entries are spread over one
group per leading value. Which later column the run fixes depends on the
query's quals, so the planner change is to build_index_paths, which has
them, as well as to BARK's scan and barkcostestimate. This is RUM's
<code>ORDER BY ts &lt;=&gt; x</code> form. A plain <code>ORDER BY
created</code> would need the planner to learn from a qual that the
extracted column is fixed, and is left out.

==== Which operator classes would need it ====

None of M2's or M5's. M5's keys are (path, value), and its sort on a path
is key order. RUM's <code>*_addon_ops</code> classes are the timestamp
example above. RUM's README describes <code>rum_anyarray_ops</code> as
storing each array's length with its elements, for the <code>&lt;=&gt;</code>
similarity ordering; this note has not checked RUM's source. A per-row
value that is carried, not ordered, is a fixed column after the extracted
one, <code>bark(tags ops, cardinality(tags))</code>. Entries of equal
(tag, length) coalesce, so it costs between a LIST's 6 bytes per member and
a SINGLE's 20, for an int4 key and an int4 length. M2 can also do without
it. With RUM's cosine-like measure, as
this note understands it, a row that matches ''m'' of the query's ''q''
elements has at least ''m'' elements, so its similarity is at most
&radic;(m/q). That bounds its distance from below, which is what
<code>xs_recheckorderby</code> and the executor's reorder queue
(IndexNextWithReorder) take, and the heap value gives the exact distance.
M2 chooses between the two; neither needs M3.

'''Upgrade path.''' If composite-key indexes of this shape turn out too
large in practice, the cheaper change is a more compact leaf format for
entries that share their leading columns, an extension of leaf prefix
compression past the first column. It keeps one key per entry, so no reader
changes. Its ceiling is the 12 bytes of header and line pointer that every
entry keeps.

== Ground rules ==

* This document is authoritative for the contracts. A commit that changes a
  contract updates this file in the same commit.
* Catalog and TOAST indexes stay on nbtree; BARK is for user indexes.
* No process-global mutable state on the BARK read/write path; annotate
  long-lived pointers per the <code>xtc</code> multithreading work.
* Every commit is independently buildable and testable (bisectable), builds
  <code>-Werror --enable-cassert</code>, and passes <code>make check</code>.
